### Thread 7 — CRAP fixes

**1. `titleHasPrToken`** — extract two named predicates:

```javascript
// Whole-token boundary predicates (gh-1075 review — CRAP ratchet): the
// loop below used to inline both tests; named helpers keep the loop's
// cyclomatic load at one branch per iteration. A trailing key char
// (digit/'-') rejects ('pr-1591' never matches 'pr-15910'); a letter/
// digit/'-'/'#' prefix rejects ('pr-1591' never matches 'gh-pr-1591-x'
// or '#pr-1591') — boundary rules mirror smAgent.stubAnchorMatches.
function isDigitOrDash(ch) {
   return (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') || ch === '-';
}

function isWordChar(ch) {
   return (ch >= 'a' && ch <= 'z') || (ch >= 'A' && ch <= 'Z') ||
       (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') || ch === '-' || ch === '#';
}

function titleHasPrToken(title, prNumber) {
   var s = String(title || '');
   var token = '[REDACTED:Sensitive Value]' + prNumber;
   var from = 0;
   while (from <= s.length - token.length) {
       var idx = s.indexOf(token, from);
       if (idx === -1) break;
       var next = s.charAt(idx + token.length);
       var prev = idx > 0 ? s.charAt(idx - 1) : '';
       if (!isDigitOrDash(next) && !isWordChar(prev)) return true;
       from = idx + 1;
   }
   return false;
}
```

Complexity of titleHasPrToken now: while + if + (|| in while cond) ≈ cyclomatic 4. CRAP with full branch coverage: low. The helpers each ~2-3. Good.

**2. `legRunOwnsPull`** — extract `isFreshActiveRun(run, nowMs, staleMs)` folding the two guards:

```javascript
/** Active AND not a stale non-running zombie — the shared freshness
 * vocabulary of both gates (gh-1028 branch + gh-1075 fork). */
function isFreshActiveRun(run, nowMs, staleMs) {
   if (!run || !isActiveStatus(run.status)) return false;
   if (run.status !== 'in_progress' && nowMs !== null &&
       isStaleNonRunning(run, nowMs, staleMs)) return false;
   return true;
}
```

And use it in both `legRunOwnsBranch` and `legRunOwnsPull`? Careful: `legRunOwnsBranch` is in the released baseline (was there pre-PR); changing it is fine as long as CRAP doesn't increase (it decreases). But REG-1 says "same-repo bookkeeping byte-identical" — that refers to behavior of the SM, not the function body. Refactoring legRunOwnsBranch to use the helper is behavior-identical. Hmm, but minimal-change principle: the reviewer asked to fold in `legRunOwnsPull` only. I could apply the helper to both (shared vocabulary) — the reviewer explicitly suggested "folding the two isActiveStatus/staleness guards into the existing shared vocabulary helpers (or a one-line isFreshActiveRun(run, nowMs, staleMs))". Using it in both places mirrors `legRunOwnsBranch`'s structure. I'll apply to both for consistency — no, wait: risk assessment. legRunOwnsBranch is covered by existing tests (8 pure tests in test_branchLegGuard). Applying the helper keeps behavior identical and reduces CRAP of both. I'll do both — cleaner, single vocabulary helper.

Actually, minimal and safest: apply in both; the helper is 3 branches. Fine.

**3. `declaredDispatchInputs`** — extract the inputs-block slicer:

Current function complexity drivers: `inDispatch` state, `inInputs` state, mInput check, mKey check, break check, indent check. Extract helper `sliceInputsBlock(lines)` that returns the lines of the `workflow_dispatch` inputs block (or null). Something like:

```javascript
// Find the workflow_dispatch `inputs:` block: returns its lines with
// baseIndent set, or null when the template has no dispatch inputs
// (the lint then fails loudly on every required key).
function dispatchInputsLines(lines) {
   var inDispatch = false;
   for (var i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
       var trimmed = lines[i].replace(/\s+$/, '');
       if (!inDispatch) {
           if (/^on:\s*$/.test(trimed)) inDispatch = true;
           continue;
       }
       var mInput = /^\s+inputs:\s*$/.exec(lines[i]);
       if (mInput) {
           var baseIndent = mInput[0].length - 'inputs:'.length - 1;
           return { start: i + 1, baseIndent: baseIndent };
       }
       // the dispatch block ends at the next top-level key
       if (/^\S/.test(trimed)) return null;
   }
   return null;
}
```

Hmm wait, the original code: once inDispatch, if not inInputs, looks for mInput and continues otherwise — never breaks on top-level keys. Actually the original loops through the whole file looking for an `inputs:` line anywhere after `on:`. E.g. `jobs:` blocks with `inputs:`? Unlikely. To keep behavior identical, helper should just scan from the `on:` line for the first `inputs:` line. Then the main function iterates from there collecting keys until dedent:

```javascript
function declaredDispatchInputs(templateText) {
   var lines = String(templateText).split(/\r?\n/);
   var block = dispatchInputsBlock(lines);  // {start, baseIndent} or null
   var inputs = {};
   if (!block) return inputs;
   for (var i = block.start; i < lines.length; i++) {
       var line = lines[i];
       var trimmed = line.replace(/\s+$/, '');
       var mKey = /^\s+([A-Za-z0-9_]+):\s*$/.exec(line);
       if (!mKey) {
           if (trimed !== '' && (line.length - line.replace(/^\s+/, '').length) <= block.baseIndent) break;
           continue;
       }
       var indent = line.length - line.replace(/^\s+/, '').length;
       if (indent > block.baseIndent) inputs[mKey[1]] = true;
   }
   return inputs;
}
```

Both helpers small. Good.

