Interesting — `stubAnchorMatches(runName, 'pr-1591')` requires the title to contain '· pr-1591'. The stub title currently never carries that token — so the gh-806/715 in-flight title latch never matched PR-anchored stub runs either (a pre-existing gap, arguably). By adding `· pr-<N>` to the stub run-name, the title latch ALSO starts working for stub fork legs — a bonus that aligns everything.

So inserting the token as ` · pr-<N>` (with the middot, matching the template's '· pr-<N>' convention) is the right format: it matches both `titleHasPrToken` (prev char '·'? wait — '·' is not a letter/digit/-/#, so prevOk=true — good) and `stubAnchorMatches` token '[REDACTED:Sensitive Value]' exactly.

Format: `format(' · pr-{0}', github.event.inputs.pr)` — renders ' · pr-1591' (spaces around ·). The template uses '· pr-{0}' within '▶ {0} (SM) · {1}'. The middot-space form matches '· pr-1591'. With ' · pr-1591' (leading space) the token '· pr-1591' is still found via indexOf. Good.

Where to insert: after the '#<n>' anchor and before the leg:
`teammate: workflow_dispatch # · pr-1591 rework`? Hmm — that renders "# · pr-1591" — slightly odd. Or after the leg: `teammate: workflow_dispatch # rework · pr-1591` — but then stubTailLegOf (regex `\s(dev|review|rework)$`) breaks → legOfRunName returns '' for the dedup module → completed-owner leg precision degrades for stub runs. The gh-1065 dedup relies on the trailing leg token. The thread explicitly warns: "preserving the trailing-leg token legOfRunName parses".

So insert before the leg. Rendered: "teammate: workflow_dispatch # · pr-1591 rework" (when issue empty) or "teammate: workflow_dispatch #1075 · pr-1591 rework" (both set). Slightly unusual but acceptable. Alternatively place it right after the '#<n>' with no space issue: `#${...}` immediately followed by `${ pr && format(' · pr-{0}') }`.

Now the lint test: add a suite in test_workflowLint.js pinning:
1. The stub run-name renders a `pr-<N>` token when inputs.pr is set (assert the run-name contains `format('· pr-{0}'` or `pr-{0}` reference and `github.event.inputs.pr`).
2. The leg token remains trailing: the run-name's final `${ }}` expression is the leg one — i.e., the title still ENDS with the leg derivation. How to pin robustly by text? Simplest: assert run-name line contains the leg expression after the pr token — i.e. index of the pr-format fragment < index of `github.event.inputs.leg`. And a pure parity check with `teammateEventDedup.legOfRunName`? That module has its own test suite; here text assertions suffice, per the thread: "A unit test pinning the stub's run-name shape".

Implementation: extract run-name value from the stub YAML text. The stub's run-name is a single-line double-quoted string. Helper in the test:

```javascript
function runNameOf(yml) {
   var m = /^run-name:\s*"(.*)"\s*$/m.exec(yml);
   return m ? m[1] : '';
}
```

Test:
```javascript
suite('workflow contract — stub run-name carries the pr anchor (gh-1075)', function () {
   test('the stub run-name renders · pr-<N> whenever inputs.pr is set', function () {
       var rn = runNameOf(STUB);
       assert.ok(rn.indexOf('github.event.inputs.pr') !== -1, ...);
       assert.ok(/format\('\s*·\s*pr-\{0\}'/.test(rn), ...);
   });
   test('the leg token stays TRAILING — legOfRunName (stubTailLegOf) must keep parsing it', function () {
       var rn = runNameOf(STUB);
       assert.ok(rn.indexOf('github.event.inputs.leg') > rn.indexOf('pr-{0}'), ...);
       // and the literal leg expression is the last ${{ }} in the title
       var lastExpr = rn.lastIndexOf('${');
       assert.ok(rn.indexOf('github.event.inputs.leg', lastExpr) !== -1, ...);
   });
```

Wait the run-name has multiple `${ }}`. The leg expression: `${ github.event.inputs.leg || (...) }}` — it's the last expression in the current stub run-name. After my change, the pr expression sits between `#...` and leg expression, so leg expr remains last. Assert: from the last `${` to the end, text contains 'github.event.inputs.leg'.

Also update the stub header comment (gh-1065 comment block above run-name) to document the pr anchor addition (gh-1075).

Also — the guard env in the dedup job uses INPUT_LEG only; unaffected.

Does test_teammateEventDedup.js parse the stub run-name? It was modified in the merge (gh-1073/1076). The stubTailLegOf tests probably use synthetic titles. Adding the pr token mid-title doesn't change titles without pr. Fine.

Also check: does anything else lint the stub run-name strictly (e.g., machine-sm or a notice)? The `foldedIfBlocks` only collects `if: >-`. OK.

### Thread 10 — file the tracking card

Create issue via dmtools github_create_issue. Title: "[GOAL] Fork leg checkout consumes inputs.checkout_ref — a fork leg must work refs/pull/<N>/head, not the dispatch ref (gh-1075 consumption follow-up)". Body describing AC1 gap, scope: leg checkout step reads inputs.checkout_ref (gh pr checkout / git fetch origin <ref>), rework push-back targets the fork per gh-1070 contract, plus lint guard. Also the self-host factory workflow (dmtools-agentic-workflows) needs the input.

Then update the template's checkout_ref description to reference the new issue. Hmm — but the description is quoted single-line YAML; adding "#107"-style ref. The issue number will be whatever GitHub assigns. Then reference it in reply.

Wait — also should the `pr` input description in the stub... not needed.

Also update `inputs.checkout_ref` description in template: currently says "consumption lives in the #1074 leg-checkout lane" — change to reference the new issue number.

### Thread 11 — park body dedup

Apply the suggested refactor in recordNoRefBookeeping.

### TDD order
Per instructions: RED first — but given practical constraints (node harness), I'll write/modify tests and run them to see them fail where applicable, then fix. For the CRAP refactor, behavior-preserving — existing tests stay green. For thread 8's test flip: update test → run (fails against old code) → fix code → green. For thread 9: add lint test → fails → fix stub. For thread 10: create issue + description update.

Let me start. First the CRAP refactors in branchLegGuard.js.

Edit 1: titleHasPrToken with two extracted predicates. Also export the new helpers? Tests may want to cover them directly... The reviewer's suggestion: "Extract two named predicates — e.g. isDigitOrDash(ch) and isWordChar(ch) (miroring smAgent.isAnchorKeyChar...). That cuts the cyclomatic load per loop iteration roughly in half; the existing 8 pure tests in test_branchLegGuard.js then cover the branches." So no new exports needed; existing tests cover. I'll keep them private (not exported) — but then nyc coverage: branchLegGuard tests exercise titleHasPrToken through hasActiveLegRunForPull with boundary cases (pr-1591 vs pr-15910 etc.) — the helpers get covered.

Edit 2: isFreshActiveRun helper, used by both legRunOwnsBranch and legRunOwnsPull.

